{
  "id": "block-2026-10-05-hypertrophy-flat-pyramid-ii",
  "number": 6,
  "name": "Hypertrophy Flat Pyramid II",
  "method": "flat-pyramid",
  "start_date": "2026-10-05",
  "weeks": 6,
  "origin": "pt",
  "summary": "Six weeks of muscle building: same weight on every set, main lifts first, then supersets of non-competing muscles. Effort builds from week 1 to 5, week 6 is lighter. More side-delt and hamstring work, and stretch-focused exercises.",
  "insights": [
    {
      "title": "Goal",
      "body": "Build muscle with moderate weights taken close to failure, after the intensity of block 5 and the lighter weeks that followed. Volume is set from your own history: more side-delt and hamstring work (your slowest-moving areas), about 18-20 weekly sets for chest and back, and every muscle gets at least one exercise that loads it in a deep stretch."
    },
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "body": "Every set of an exercise uses the same weight and reps. The main lifts (30-degree incline dumbbell press, chest-supported T-bar row, Smith machine shoulder press, pendulum squat) come first, done alone with 2.5-3 minutes rest. The rest are supersets of muscles that don't compete, like flat dumbbell press with underhand pulldowns or incline curls with overhead triceps extensions: first exercise, about 10 seconds, second exercise, then the full rest. Abs moved to arms days so leg days stay short."
    },
    {
      "title": "A days vs B days",
      "body": "A days are heavier: 8 reps on the main lifts, 10 on the other compound lifts, 12 on isolation exercises and 15 on lateral raises, rear delts and calves. B days repeat the same exercises in the same order with lighter weights for 10, 12, 15 and 20 reps. Track and progress A and B days separately."
    },
    {
      "title": "How to progress",
      "body": "The reps shown are your target. When every set reaches it with clean form at that week's effort, add the smallest step next time (about 1-2.5 kg upper body, 2.5-5 kg legs); if the last sets fall 1-2 reps short, keep the weight and beat it next week. Effort rises through the block: week 1 stop about 3 reps before failure, week 2 about 2, weeks 3-4 about 1-2, week 5 about 1, and only the last set of machine, cable or isolation work may go to failure."
    },
    {
      "title": "What to expect",
      "body": "Week 1 should feel easy on purpose while you find your weights and learn the new exercises. Weeks 2-5 get harder each week and should bring more reps or weight on most lifts. Week 6 is lighter: same exercises and weights, but only half the sets (round up) and stop about 3 reps before failure, so you start the heavy block 7 fresh."
    },
    {
      "title": "Key tips",
      "body": "Set up stations before supersets, for example a flat bench next to the pulldown. Lower slowly into the stretch on every press, fly, curl, RDL and calf raise, without bouncing. Never grind heavy squats, RDLs or presses to failure. If a movement hurts, stop it and see a qualified professional."
    }
  ],
